%DocsPage
  %PageHeader{
    kicker: "Plugin Reference",
    title: "Test Plugin",
    summary: "Klenod::Test::Plugin discovers test entrypoints and keeps them outside the application import graph."
  }

  %DocsSection{ title: "Configuration" }
    :markdown
      Add the plugin to the same development configuration as the application plugins:

      ```ruby
      Klenod::Test::Plugin.new(
        pattern: "**/*.test.rb"
      )
      ```

      `pattern` is a source-relative glob. It defaults to `**/*.test.rb`.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Test Boundaries" }
    :markdown
      Test files are independent entrypoints. They can import Ruby, Haml, CSS, data, assets, and other application modules through the normal plugin graph. Application modules and tests cannot import a file matched by the test pattern. Shared test helpers should therefore use ordinary `.rb` filenames.

      Normal builds do not discover test entrypoints, so tests are absent from production bundles.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Selecting Related Tests" }
    :markdown
      `Klenod::Test::Suite` discovers tests through the plugin and collects their complete static dependency closures without evaluating them:

      ```ruby
      suite = Klenod::Test::Suite.new(
        context: context,
        plugin: test_plugin
      )

      initial = suite.collect
      related = suite.update(event)
      ```

      Both results expose sorted `test_paths` and `removed_test_paths`. A framework decides how to evaluate those paths and which test library reports the results.

      The `klenod-test` package combines these APIs with the Klenod watcher and fresh worker processes. The `klenod` meta-gem exposes them as `klenod test` and `klenod coverage`. Both delegate execution to callbacks from `klenod.test.rb`, so they do not depend on Minitest or another testing library.

